<Base title="Method">
  <h1>Method</h1>
  <p class="lede">Every claim on this site carries a confidence and a source. The point of saying so is
  that it lets a claim be overturned.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="chip doc">documented</span></td>
        <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan itself.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="chip">inferred</span></td>
        <td>A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so it can be argued with.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="chip dis">disputed</span></td>
        <td>Asserted somewhere in the family record, but not supported — and sometimes contradicted — by what can be seen.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="chip">family lore</span></td>
        <td>Told and remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because pretending
        otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The case against Agostino Falcone</h2>
  <p>The line is commonly carried one generation above Pasquale Falco, to an <strong>Agostino Falcone</strong>
  born about 1740 and a wife <strong>Elisabetta Sabina</strong>. This archive does not assert that link. The
  reasons:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The surname is different.</strong> Not <em>Falco</em> but <em>Falcone</em> — a related name,
    but a distinct one.</li>
    <li><strong>Falcone is not an Arienzo surname.</strong> Across 361 pages of Arienzo baptism registers
    that mention a Falco, <em>Falcone</em> appears twice, both times in a list of names on an index page.
    <em>Crisci</em> appears on 113 pages, <em>Morgillo</em> on 76. A family present in the parish leaves a
    trace of that order. Falcone did not.</li>
    <li><strong>The one Falcone found in a register is a priest.</strong> In the baptism entries the word
    appears as the officiant — the priest by whose licence the child was baptised — not as a parent.</li>
    <li><strong>No record has been produced for either him or his wife.</strong> The generations below all
    carry citations; these two carry none.</li>
    <li><strong>The search index conflates the two names.</strong> FamilySearch returns Falco and Falcone
    for either query. A researcher working through that index can very easily acquire a Falcone ancestor
    without ever choosing to.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>None of this proves Agostino Falcone did not exist, and none of it proves he was not an ancestor. It
  establishes only that the claim is presently unsupported, and that the honest place to put the frontier
  of this pedigree is at Pasquale Falco.</p>

  <h2>On dates read by machine</h2>
  <p>Much of what is new here was found by handwriting recognition applied to registers no one has indexed
  by hand. It is a remarkable tool and it is wrong about digits more often than about names. Where a
  machine-read date is load-bearing it is flagged, and it should be confirmed against the image before it
  is believed. One such case is on the <a href="./arienzo/">Arienzo</a> page.</p>

  <h2>Living people</h2>
  <p>Living people are <strong>omitted from this build entirely</strong> — not hidden, not gated, not present
  in the output. A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.</p>
</Base>
